	affiliations = {Department of Electronics and Telecommunication Engineering, Shah & Anchor Kutchhi Engineering College, Maharashtra, Mumbai, India},
	abstract = {Automated code review and software quality evaluation have become central concerns in modern software engineering, where rapid development cycles and large, continuously evolving codebases strain traditional manual quality assurance. This survey synthesises research from 2020 to 2026 on AI-driven techniques for code review and software quality evaluation, focusing on two complementary paradigms: Automated Machine Learning (AutoML) and Large Language Models (LLMs). AutoML methods—including neural architecture search (NAS), hyperparameter optimisation (HPO), and meta-learning—automatically construct and optimise predictive models for defect prediction, bug localisation, and code smell detection, eliminating the need for manual feature engineering and expert configuration. Foundation models and LLMs pre-trained on large code corpora—CodeBERT, GraphCodeBERT, CodeT5, Codex, Code Llama, and instruction-tuned conversational agents such as ChatGPT and GPT-4—have demonstrated strong capabilities in code understanding, generation, and review assistance, enabling automated review-comment generation, vulnerability detection, and refactoring suggestions, while still requiring careful calibration and human verification for deployment-critical use. We organise the literature along a task taxonomy encompassing defect and vulnerability prediction, code smell and maintainability assessment, review-comment generation, and DevOps analytics, and systematically compare AutoML-driven and LLM-driven techniques across effectiveness, scalability, and interpretability. Multi-modal learning and human–AI collaboration are highlighted as emerging dimensions of state-of-the-art systems. Empirical benchmarks including Defects4J, Big-Vul, CodeXGLUE, SWE-bench, and NPRComment are reviewed to characterise evaluation practices and reproducibility norms. Critical open challenges—scaling to real-time CI/CD, cross-project generalisation, developer-legible explanations, and trust calibration—are identified, and a structured six-point research agenda is outlined. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Science+Business Media, LLC, part of Springer Nature 2026.},
	author_keywords = {Automated code review; AutoML; Code intelligence; Continuous integration; Defect prediction; Foundation models; Human–AI collaboration; Hyperparameter optimisation; Large language models; Neural architecture search; Program analysis; Software quality assurance; Technical debt; Vulnerability detection},
