	abstract = {Code review in pull requests (PRs) acts as a mechanism for defect detection, knowledge sharing, and renegotiation of architectural decisions. The adoption of generative agents based on LLMs in this flow advances rapidly, but an empirical gap persists regarding how developers perceive these tools in real industrial contexts. This paper reports an exploratory case study of Gandalf, a generative code-review agent that operates on GitHub PRs, deployed at Brisanet Telecomunicações S.A., a large Brazilian telecommunications company across multiple stacks. A mixed-methods study was conducted with 17 respondents, using a questionnaire based in the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM) extended to include trust in AI, and open-ended questions, which were analyzed using thematic coding, complemented by descriptive operational data from four months of the deployment. The findings indicate high self-reported acceptance (Behavioral Intention 4.37) and perceived usefulness concentrated at the PR level (Perceived Usefulness 3.92), a dominant posture of calibrated trust with human validation (8/17), and a perceived architectural impact that is indirect, limited by the absence of context beyond the diff (context-blindness in 10/17). The contributions include an industrial perception study of a diff-only LLM review agent, with a documented technical description of the agent; empirical evidence of calibrated trust; and practical requirements, articulated as design implications for future work (context, memory, and tiered analysis), for evolving from diff-level toward architecture-aware review. © 2026 The Authors},
