	affiliations = {School of Computer Science and Engineering, University of Electronic Science and Technology of China, Sichuan, China; School of Computer Science, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, Shanghai, China},
	abstract = {Static taint analysis is central to vulnerability detection. In real-world taint analysis, accurate vulnerability detection requires both semantic models for external library APIs and well-defined source and sink specifications. Existing approaches address these requirements by applying program analysis to model external library APIs and using Large Language Models (LLMs) or manually inferring the source and sink specifications. However, current external library modeling approaches typically require access to the source code, compiled artifacts, or API specifications of external libraries. Concurrently, while LLMs can be used to identify sources and sinks automatically, existing methods still require the analysis of a large number of security-irrelevant functions, resulting in unnecessary computational overhead. We present CPGHunter, a framework that integrates LLM semantic reasoning with static analysis. It introduces two key innovations to address the challenges of external library API modeling and scalable source and sink identification. First, it uses LLMs to automatically model data-flow semantics for external library APIs, eliminating the need for prior access to their source code or documentation. Second, it employs semantic package screening to narrow the scope of LLM-based source and sink identification, thereby reducing the analysis of security-irrelevant functions. We evaluate CPGHunter using the Juliet Test Suite for C and Java, as well as 20 real-world CVEs from the CWE-Bench-Java dataset. On the Juliet C/C++ test suite, CPGHunter shows clear advantages on CWE-78 and CWE-90 where CodeQL struggles due to incomplete models for external libraries. On the Juliet Java test suite, CPGHunter achieves performance comparable to CodeQL with a slightly lower F1-score. On the real-world CVEs, CPGHunter achieves a higher precision than the baseline (0.287 vs. 0.131) and a better F1-score (0.375 vs. 0.223). © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Science+Business Media, LLC, part of Springer Nature 2026.},
	author_keywords = {Code property graph; Large language models; Taint analysis; Vulnerability detection},
