	abstract = {As software complexity grows, security vulnerabilities become more frequent and harmful, making automated vulnerability detection essential. While recent LLM-based detectors combine semantic reasoning with static analysis for project-scale scanning, their real-world effectiveness remains unclear, and the underlying root causes of their failures are still underexplored. In this paper, we present the first comprehensive empirical study of specialized LLM-based detectors and compare them with traditional static analyzers at the project scale. Specifically, our study evaluates five of the latest and representative specialized LLM-based methods, two general Agent-based methods and four traditional tools using: 1) a controlled benchmark of 265 known real-world vulnerabilities (C/C++ and Java) to assess detection capability, and 2) 24 active open-source projects/modules, where we analyzed 6,442 sampled warnings using Codex-assisted labeling with stratified manual validation and classified the root causes of 5,896 false positives using a taxonomy derived from 355 manually inspected reports. Our evaluation yields three key findings: First, general agent methods achieve the highest recall, but still struggle with complex code. And both LLM-based and traditional methods suffer from incomplete API modeling. Second, in open-source projects/modules, both LLM-based and traditional tools generate substantial warnings but suffer from very high false discovery rates, hindering practical use. Our analysis further reveals truncated interprocedural context and incorrect program-point classification as primary failure causes, with LLM-based tools exhibiting additional unique failures. Finally, LLM-based methods incur substantial computational costs?hundreds of thousands to hundreds of millions of tokens (cost up to more than $1,000) and multi-hour to multi-day runtimes. Overall, our findings underscore critical limitations in the robustness, reliability, and scalability of current LLM-based detectors. We ultimately summarize a set of implications for future research toward more effective and practical project-scale vulnerability detection. © 1976-2012 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {Empirical study; Large language model; Vulnerability detection},
	keywords = {Application programming interfaces (API); Autonomous agents; C (programming language); Computational methods; Network security; Open source software; Open systems; Scalability; Static analysis; Empirical studies; Language model; Large language model; Open source projects; Project scale; Real-world; Root cause; Security vulnerabilities; Software complexity; Vulnerability detection; Semantics},
