	abstract = {Operational Technology (OT) systems, which form the control and actuation layer of Industrial Cyber-Physical Systems (ICPS), integrate embedded control software in safety-critical environments where reliability and cybersecurity underpin overall system trustworthiness. Despite growing interest in Artificial Intelligence (AI)-assisted assessment, reproducible and standards-aligned benchmarks for evaluating cybersecurity risk reasoning in OT software remain limited. This paper introduces EmbSoftOTBench, a standards-aligned benchmark that operationalizes IEC 62443-3-2 through machine-readable scenarios derived from MITRE EMB3D. The benchmark provides paired threat-benign scenarios with quantitative ILVE factors (Impact, Likelihood, Vulnerability, Exposure) and expert rationales, enabling systematic and reproducible evaluation of mitigation-aware cybersecurity risk reasoning rather than vulnerability detection alone. An experimental evaluation using state-of-the-art Large Language Models (LLMs) reveals near-chance classification accuracy on threat/benign discrimination, strong sensitivity to prompt structure, and systematic trade-offs between sensitivity, specificity, calibration, and auditability. These results indicate that current LLMs are not suitable for autonomous OT risk assessment but can support constrained, assistive, and audit-ready workflows. The dataset is publicly released under the CC BY 4.0 license to support reproducible research and standards-based validation in trustworthy ICPS environments.  © 2026 IEEE.},
